Title: THE LONG WAY DOWN
Credit: Written by
Author: D. Jerkovic
Draft date: 2026-08-02
Notes: Original fixture written for Stripboard testing. Contains four deliberately
Notes: planted continuity errors — see fixtures/ground_truth.json. Not a real film.

====

INT. ROADSIDE DINER - DAY

Formica the colour of old teeth. Rain hammers the window.

RENA MARCH, 30s, sits alone in the corner booth in a cracked
LEATHER JACKET. She turns a chipped COFFEE CUP a slow
half-circle. Doesn't drink it.

The BELL over the door goes. She doesn't look up.

DELACROIX, 60s, heavy coat, sits down opposite her uninvited.

DELACROIX
You look like a woman waiting on
bad news.

RENA
I'm waiting on a bus.

DELACROIX
Bus came twenty minutes ago.

Rena finally looks at him.

EXT. DINER PARKING LOT - NIGHT

The '67 MUSTANG idles under a dead streetlight, wipers going.
Its paint is oxidised to the colour of dried blood.

Rena crosses the wet asphalt. Delacroix watches from the
doorway, not following.

INT. MUSTANG (MOVING) - NIGHT

Rena drives one-handed. On the passenger seat: a BRASS KEY on
a loop of green string.

She picks it up. Turns it over. Puts it back.

EXT. HIGHWAY 9 - NIGHT

The Mustang, small against the dark, heading north.

INT. MOTEL ROOM 12 - NIGHT

Bare bulb. Still in the LEATHER JACKET, Rena unlocks the metal
FOOTLOCKER at the end of the bed with the BRASS KEY.

Inside: banded cash, a passport, and a REVOLVER she does not
touch.

She takes the passport. Closes the locker.

INT. MOTEL ROOM 12 - CONTINUOUS

Rena sits on the bed's edge in the DENIM JACKET, passport open.
The face in it is hers. The name is not.

INT. MOTEL CORRIDOR - DAY

Rena walks the corridor, passport in her coat. It is past
midnight and the building is dead quiet.

A CLEANER, 50s, pushes a trolley past her without looking up.

EXT. MOTEL FORECOURT - DAY

Grey light. Rena stops. Looks back at the room she just left.

INT. PAWN SHOP - DAY

Cluttered. Behind the counter, VOSS, 40s, examines the BRASS
KEY under a loupe.

VOSS
Where'd you find this?

RENA
Does it matter?

VOSS
It matters to the man who lost it.

Voss sets the key down between them. Neither picks it up.

EXT. PAWN SHOP - DAY

Rena walks out fast. Behind her, through the glass, Voss picks
up a phone.

EXT. LOADING DOCK - DAY

Delacroix is waiting. He presses the BRASS KEY into Rena's
palm, closing her fingers over it.

DELACROIX
First time you've held it. Locker
twelve. Don't lose it.

She looks at the key like she has never seen it before.

EXT. DINER PARKING LOT - NIGHT

The '68 MUSTANG is gone. Only oil on the asphalt where it
stood, and the rain thinning it out.

Delacroix stands in the empty space with his hands in his
pockets.

DELACROIX
(to nobody)
Long way down.

INT. ROADSIDE DINER - NIGHT

Rena in the same corner booth, in a faded DENIM JACKET. The
COFFEE CUP is in front of her, full, untouched.

She is not waiting on a bus.

FADE OUT.
